WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:05.141
This briefing comes from Book 14 of
the Federal Modernization Workbook,

3
00:00:05.141 --> 00:00:07.549
Federal Compliance and Authorization.

4
00:00:07.549 --> 00:00:10.217
It's written for authorizing officials,

5
00:00:10.217 --> 00:00:13.450
information system security
officers and CIOs.

6
00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:14.863
I'll take four questions.

7
00:00:14.863 --> 00:00:18.103
Why does every control need
proof rather than a promise,

8
00:00:18.103 --> 00:00:20.638
and what is the federal
clock asking for now?

9
00:00:20.638 --> 00:00:21.876
What do the baseline,

10
00:00:21.876 --> 00:00:25.737
the closure rule and the agency-level
control families require?

11
00:00:25.737 --> 00:00:27.176
Who owns compliance?

12
00:00:27.176 --> 00:00:30.588
And how do you build the authorization
package and keep it current?

13
00:00:30.588 --> 00:00:34.178
If you remember one thing, make
it this: a control is closed when

14
00:00:34.178 --> 00:00:38.050
the thing that closes it is running,
and the record shows it working.

15
00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:41.338
Everything else is a
promise, and promises age.

16
00:00:41.338 --> 00:00:43.840
Let's start with who owes which proof.

17
00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:48.603
Compliance usually gets described
as a long list of obligations.

18
00:00:48.603 --> 00:00:52.068
What an authorizing official
actually needs is narrower:

19
00:00:52.068 --> 00:00:56.690
to know each control is working today,
and to show anyone who asks how they know.

20
00:00:56.690 --> 00:00:59.240
That's the difference
between a promise and proof.

21
00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:01.586
A promise is a line in the security plan,

22
00:01:01.586 --> 00:01:05.765
backed by evidence someone gathered
by hand for the last assessment.

23
00:01:05.765 --> 00:01:09.553
It was true the day it was signed,
and it's been aging ever since.

24
00:01:09.553 --> 00:01:12.299
Proof is a record the
system produces itself,

25
00:01:12.299 --> 00:01:15.640
one another reviewer can
check and get the same answer.

26
00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:16.890
So who owes what?

27
00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:20.403
The cloud provider proves its
service under FedRAMP's rules.

28
00:01:20.403 --> 00:01:24.364
The agency proves its own system
and its side of the boundary.

29
00:01:24.364 --> 00:01:29.077
A provider's package is presumed
adequate, but the agency still authorizes

30
00:01:29.077 --> 00:01:33.940
its own system, and only its authorizing
official accepts the remaining risk.

31
00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:35.650
Next, the clock.

32
00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.539
The dates driving this
work come from FedRAMP,

33
00:01:39.539 --> 00:01:42.925
CISA and OMB, and each one
binds a different party.

34
00:01:42.925 --> 00:01:46.771
Every date on the slide was checked
against its issuing document,

35
00:01:46.771 --> 00:01:51.400
and where a document gives a period instead
of a date, the date is counted from it.

36
00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:57.488
The nearest one is the post-quantum
migration plan, due October 22, 2026.

37
00:01:57.488 --> 00:02:04.048
Logging plans, CISA's remediation timelines
and FedRAMP's vulnerability rules follow

38
00:02:04.048 --> 00:02:05.845
over the next few months,

39
00:02:05.845 --> 00:02:10.249
and FedRAMP's 2026 rules become
mandatory on January 1, 2027.

40
00:02:10.249 --> 00:02:14.650
Further out, there's
TLS 1.3 support by 2030.

41
00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:16.075
Some things also moved.

42
00:02:16.075 --> 00:02:19.438
Software bills of materials
became a contract choice,

43
00:02:19.438 --> 00:02:23.100
but the software and hardware
inventory is still required.

44
00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:25.306
The practical advice is simple:

45
00:02:25.306 --> 00:02:29.038
keep one dated register, and
re-verify it at every gate.

46
00:02:29.038 --> 00:02:31.420
Now, what does Moderate actually mean?

47
00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:35.647
Most civilian cloud systems
are authorized at Moderate,

48
00:02:35.647 --> 00:02:37.570
and that word is doing real work.

49
00:02:37.570 --> 00:02:41.310
The agency rates the impact
of losing confidentiality,

50
00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:45.132
integrity and availability,
and the highest rating wins.

51
00:02:45.132 --> 00:02:49.532
If nothing is high and at least one thing
is moderate, it's a Moderate system.

52
00:02:49.532 --> 00:02:52.602
The agency then tailors
NIST's Moderate baseline,

53
00:02:52.602 --> 00:02:55.671
which is the size of the
promise that system makes.

54
00:02:55.671 --> 00:02:58.407
FedRAMP's Class C is a separate fact.

55
00:02:58.407 --> 00:03:02.398
It includes the Moderate baseline,
but it lists more controls,

56
00:03:02.398 --> 00:03:05.371
a few dozen of them outside
NIST's Moderate set.

57
00:03:05.371 --> 00:03:07.108
So cite the two separately.

58
00:03:07.108 --> 00:03:10.301
A Moderate system built on
a Class C service inherits

59
00:03:10.301 --> 00:03:13.132
the provider's evidence
for the provider's side.

60
00:03:13.132 --> 00:03:17.571
It still has to prove its own side,
against its own tailored baseline.

61
00:03:17.571 --> 00:03:21.860
Which raises the real question:
when is a control actually closed?

62
00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:24.035
Is a control closed?

63
00:03:24.035 --> 00:03:28.618
A program that can't answer precisely
answers loosely: the product was bought,

64
00:03:28.618 --> 00:03:32.485
the vendor announced the feature,
the ticket was marked complete.

65
00:03:32.485 --> 00:03:34.685
None of those is the control operating.

66
00:03:34.685 --> 00:03:37.610
Book 14 applies two tests, in order.

67
00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:41.023
First, the mechanism exists:
it's deployed, running,

68
00:03:41.023 --> 00:03:44.685
and reaches the part of the
estate the control covers.

69
00:03:44.685 --> 00:03:47.210
A purchase order or a roadmap fails.

70
00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:49.281
Second, its proof is recorded:

71
00:03:49.281 --> 00:03:53.510
a machine-readable verification,
a time, and what checked it.

72
00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:58.822
Fail the first test and the control is open,
with a milestone in the plan of action.

73
00:03:58.822 --> 00:04:02.027
Pass the first but fail
the second and it's worse:

74
00:04:02.027 --> 00:04:04.573
it looks closed and can't be shown to be.

75
00:04:04.573 --> 00:04:07.023
And a vendor roadmap is not a commitment.

76
00:04:07.023 --> 00:04:11.197
Date every claim, and write the
capability into the contract.

77
00:04:11.197 --> 00:04:14.530
Next, the families no
provider can close for you.

78
00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:19.188
Some control families no cloud
provider can ever close for you,

79
00:04:19.188 --> 00:04:21.442
and program management is the first.

80
00:04:21.442 --> 00:04:25.008
NIST treats the whole family
as organization-wide,

81
00:04:25.008 --> 00:04:29.931
outside any system baseline, and
FedRAMP doesn't assign it to any class.

82
00:04:29.931 --> 00:04:31.820
It comes from FISMA and OMB,

83
00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:36.630
and it binds the agency whether or
not it uses a single cloud service.

84
00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:41.518
No tool can write an organization's risk
tolerance or appoint its senior official.

85
00:04:41.518 --> 00:04:44.361
These controls close when
the artifact exists,

86
00:04:44.361 --> 00:04:48.880
a named official has approved it, and
the agency can show it's still followed.

87
00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:53.371
Think of the security program plan,
the appointed senior security officer,

88
00:04:53.371 --> 00:04:57.718
a signed risk tolerance, and one
organization-wide monitoring strategy.

89
00:04:57.718 --> 00:05:02.456
One distinction matters to an assessor:
the program inventory lists systems,

90
00:05:02.456 --> 00:05:05.492
and each system's inventory
lists what's inside it.

91
00:05:05.492 --> 00:05:06.768
The two must agree.

92
00:05:06.768 --> 00:05:09.340
Two more families close the same way.

93
00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:15.340
Contingency planning and supply chain also
close through governance, not technology.

94
00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:18.965
Contingency planning asks
for a plan, not a capability.

95
00:05:18.965 --> 00:05:22.538
You can have tested failover,
two circuits and good backups,

96
00:05:22.538 --> 00:05:27.423
and still carry an open finding, because
nothing says who declares a contingency,

97
00:05:27.423 --> 00:05:31.215
who gets told, what comes back first,
or when you're back to normal.

98
00:05:31.215 --> 00:05:34.553
And the mission owner, not
the database administrator,

99
00:05:34.553 --> 00:05:36.853
sets the maximum tolerable downtime.

100
00:05:36.853 --> 00:05:40.390
For a Moderate system, NIST
recommends a real exercise

101
00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:43.377
with a restore from backup,
not just a tabletop.

102
00:05:43.377 --> 00:05:45.277
Supply chain shifted this year.

103
00:05:45.277 --> 00:05:48.746
The secure-development
attestation mandate was rescinded,

104
00:05:48.746 --> 00:05:52.077
and software bills of materials
became a contract choice.

105
00:05:52.077 --> 00:05:54.356
But the inventory is still required,

106
00:05:54.356 --> 00:05:58.078
and supply chain policy and
controls are in every baseline.

107
00:05:58.078 --> 00:06:00.782
Providers report their vulnerabilities;

108
00:06:00.782 --> 00:06:04.490
the agency reads those reports
into its own plan of action.

109
00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:06.880
Next, privacy and training.

110
00:06:07.630 --> 00:06:11.393
Privacy and training belong
to the agency, end to end.

111
00:06:11.393 --> 00:06:14.580
The privacy baseline applies
at every impact level,

112
00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:18.436
so a Low system holding the same
personal information carries

113
00:06:18.436 --> 00:06:22.292
the same privacy controls, and
FedRAMP doesn't cover any of them.

114
00:06:22.292 --> 00:06:24.999
The questions are about
authority and purpose:

115
00:06:24.999 --> 00:06:28.884
why the agency collects the information,
whether it told the person,

116
00:06:28.884 --> 00:06:32.006
and whether it published the
system of records notice.

117
00:06:32.006 --> 00:06:36.730
The senior privacy official approves
the privacy plan before authorization.

118
00:06:36.730 --> 00:06:40.555
Training is the one family
no configuration can close.

119
00:06:40.555 --> 00:06:45.155
Training comes before access, and
awareness is refreshed at least yearly.

120
00:06:45.155 --> 00:06:48.071
Here's the trap: a dashboard
showing ninety-eight

121
00:06:48.071 --> 00:06:51.205
percent completion is a
number with no denominator.

122
00:06:51.205 --> 00:06:55.218
The proof is counts per population,
measured against a stated roster,

123
00:06:55.218 --> 00:06:58.105
that someone who didn't
produce them can re-derive.

124
00:06:58.105 --> 00:07:00.100
So who owns all of this?

125
00:07:00.850 --> 00:07:04.933
Ask who owns compliance and the
answer is often the security office,

126
00:07:04.933 --> 00:07:07.412
which really means nobody in particular.

127
00:07:07.412 --> 00:07:11.224
The Risk Management Framework
names an owner for every task.

128
00:07:11.224 --> 00:07:15.883
The system owner carries the most:
categorizing, implementing controls,

129
00:07:15.883 --> 00:07:20.463
fixing findings, keeping the plan of
action, and assembling the package.

130
00:07:20.463 --> 00:07:24.738
The CISO sets requirements and the
common controls systems inherit.

131
00:07:24.738 --> 00:07:28.800
The privacy official approves anything
touching personal information.

132
00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:32.634
The authorizing official is the
only one who can accept the risk,

133
00:07:32.634 --> 00:07:34.225
and that can't be delegated.

134
00:07:34.225 --> 00:07:38.925
They also approve the plans and select
the assessor before the assessment starts.

135
00:07:38.925 --> 00:07:43.340
Several of these roles are government-only,
so a contractor-heavy team

136
00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:47.450
can prepare every plan and package,
but can't sign those decisions.

137
00:07:47.450 --> 00:07:51.062
And keep the roles apart:
nobody assesses their own work.

138
00:07:51.062 --> 00:07:53.280
Now, the package itself.

139
00:07:54.030 --> 00:07:57.443
The authorizing official doesn't
read the evidence directly.

140
00:07:57.443 --> 00:08:01.913
They read a package that summarizes
it: the security and privacy plans,

141
00:08:01.913 --> 00:08:06.229
the assessment reports, the plan of
action, and an executive summary.

142
00:08:06.229 --> 00:08:11.049
NIST says the package may be generated
from a tool, and FedRAMP's 2026

143
00:08:11.049 --> 00:08:15.955
rules now expect machine-readable
artifacts, at least OSCAL and JSON.

144
00:08:15.955 --> 00:08:20.683
In OSCAL the pieces link together: the
assessment plan points to the security plan,

145
00:08:20.683 --> 00:08:23.047
the results point to the assessment plan,

146
00:08:23.047 --> 00:08:25.967
and the plan of action hangs
from the security plan.

147
00:08:25.967 --> 00:08:28.506
Every failing item gets a disposition.

148
00:08:28.506 --> 00:08:32.255
Real drift is an open item
with an owner and a deadline.

149
00:08:32.255 --> 00:08:37.430
An accepted exception carries its expiry,
and when that lapses, it's open again.

150
00:08:37.430 --> 00:08:39.255
Nothing is always satisfied.

151
00:08:39.255 --> 00:08:41.851
And an empty plan of action
on a Moderate system

152
00:08:41.851 --> 00:08:44.780
should make you ask what the
assessment didn't look at.

153
00:08:44.780 --> 00:08:46.940
Next, keeping it current.

154
00:08:47.690 --> 00:08:51.128
A package describes the system
on the day it was generated.

155
00:08:51.128 --> 00:08:53.852
Continuous monitoring
keeps that day recent.

156
00:08:53.852 --> 00:08:56.923
The law sets the floor:
testing at least annually,

157
00:08:56.923 --> 00:08:59.303
and there's no federal three-year rule.

158
00:08:59.303 --> 00:09:02.718
Once monitoring is mature,
ongoing authorization

159
00:09:02.718 --> 00:09:06.052
replaces a fixed end date
with a review frequency.

160
00:09:06.052 --> 00:09:09.615
The program's own cadence is
monthly, quarterly and annual.

161
00:09:09.615 --> 00:09:12.357
Each month, regenerate
the results and plan of

162
00:09:12.357 --> 00:09:15.677
action and report posture to
the authorizing official.

163
00:09:15.677 --> 00:09:19.169
Each quarter, review every
provider's ongoing report,

164
00:09:19.169 --> 00:09:21.628
with a named senior official doing it.

165
00:09:21.628 --> 00:09:25.727
Each year, an independent assessor
re-assesses every control.

166
00:09:25.727 --> 00:09:29.602
One caution: vendor posture scores
aren't assessment evidence.

167
00:09:29.602 --> 00:09:33.152
A score measures progress against
a vendor's recommendations.

168
00:09:33.152 --> 00:09:37.866
An assessment result says whether an
objective is satisfied, and on what evidence.

169
00:09:37.866 --> 00:09:39.800
Let's step back to the map.

170
00:09:40.550 --> 00:09:44.412
The older Federal Compliance series
arranged its material by layer.

171
00:09:44.412 --> 00:09:47.594
The workbook arranges it by
the question a reader brings,

172
00:09:47.594 --> 00:09:51.588
and a chapter maps each part of the
series to the book that carries it now.

173
00:09:51.588 --> 00:09:55.351
Where they disagree, the book
is the later, footnoted text.

174
00:09:55.351 --> 00:09:57.350
Two ideas explain the order.

175
00:09:57.350 --> 00:10:02.087
Naming and identity come first, because
you can't inventory what you can't name.

176
00:10:02.087 --> 00:10:04.773
Assurance comes last,
because the package can

177
00:10:04.773 --> 00:10:07.812
only report what the earlier
books made observable.

178
00:10:07.812 --> 00:10:11.600
And even after you build everything
on the map, risk remains.

179
00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:15.107
The slide lists six kinds,
from self-assessment that no

180
00:10:15.107 --> 00:10:19.550
independent assessor has confirmed
yet, to features not yet shipped.

181
00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:20.963
Write that remainder down.

182
00:10:20.963 --> 00:10:23.704
A package that says
what's proven, asserted,

183
00:10:23.704 --> 00:10:28.562
inherited and accepted gives the
authorizing official a risk they can see.

184
00:10:28.562 --> 00:10:30.650
That leads to the decisions.

185
00:10:31.400 --> 00:10:32.775
So, four decisions.

186
00:10:32.775 --> 00:10:35.803
Name the owners in writing,
with signed appointments

187
00:10:35.803 --> 00:10:38.900
and the authorizing official
selecting the assessor.

188
00:10:38.900 --> 00:10:43.049
Keep one dated clock: submit
the post-quantum migration plan,

189
00:10:43.049 --> 00:10:47.363
and re-check every federal date
against its source at each gate.

190
00:10:47.363 --> 00:10:51.825
Fund the mechanisms that produce records
before the tools that measure them,

191
00:10:51.825 --> 00:10:55.800
because assessment tools confirm a
design; they can't stand in for one.

192
00:10:55.800 --> 00:10:59.564
And generate the package, with
a disposition on every failing

193
00:10:59.564 --> 00:11:03.475
item and a named reviewer for
every provider's quarterly report.

194
00:11:03.475 --> 00:11:05.675
The next step needs no procurement.

195
00:11:05.675 --> 00:11:10.312
Fill in the product inventory questionnaire
against the estate as it is today,

196
00:11:10.312 --> 00:11:13.737
and settle every flag before
the security plan is written.

197
00:11:13.737 --> 00:11:16.176
A promise is true on the day it's signed.

198
00:11:16.176 --> 00:11:17.900
Proof keeps showing itself.

199
00:11:17.900 --> 00:11:21.975
Book 14 has the detail,
with every claim footnoted.

200
00:11:21.975 --> 00:11:22.810
Thank you.
